# Strength and certainty of graded recommendations in ESICM, SCCM and ACP clinical practice guidelines, {{ mf.search.start[:4] }}–{{ mf.search.cutoff_year }}: an automated, reproducible analysis

*Draft generated from analysis package `{{ analysis.package }}`, snapshot {{ analysis.snapshot.id }}, extractor version `{{ analysis.extractor.version_id[:12] }}`. Do not edit numbers here; edit the template.*

## Key Points

**Question** How strong are the recommendations of three major critical care and internal medicine societies, and how certain is the evidence behind them?

**Findings** In this cross-sectional study of {{ t.ALL.n_graded }} graded recommendations in {{ flow.current.all }} current guidelines, {{ t.ALL.strong_pct | pct }}% were strong and {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% rested on high or moderate certainty evidence; {{ strong_low_pct | pct }}% of strong recommendations rested on low or very low certainty evidence. A locally run language model extracted recommendations with F1 {{ held.f1 | p3 }} on held-out guidelines.

**Meaning** Most recommendations in these guidelines rest on low or very low certainty evidence; a reproducible extraction pipeline allows this audit to be repeated as guidelines change.

## Abstract

**Importance** The GRADE approach separates the strength of a recommendation from the certainty of the evidence behind it. How often critical care and internal medicine guidelines make strong recommendations on low certainty evidence is not routinely tracked, and manual audits are not repeated when guidelines are updated.

**Objective** To describe the strength, direction and certainty of evidence of graded recommendations in guidelines of the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine (ESICM), the Society of Critical Care Medicine (SCCM) and the American College of Physicians (ACP), using an automated and reproducible extraction pipeline.

**Design, Setting, and Participants** Cross-sectional study of clinical practice guidelines led or co-led by ESICM, SCCM or ACP and first published online from {{ mf.search.start }} to {{ mf.search.cutoff }}, with at least one recommendation graded for both strength and certainty. We included {{ flow.included.all }} guidelines ({{ flow.current.all }} current editions). A locally run large language model extracted each recommendation and its grade; duplicates were removed and grades harmonized to GRADE.

**Exposure** None (descriptive study); results are stratified by issuing society.

**Main Outcomes and Measures** Proportions of graded recommendations that were strong, that recommended against an intervention, and that rested on high or moderate certainty evidence, with guideline-cluster bootstrap 95% CIs. Extraction accuracy was measured on {{ dev.n_guidelines }} labelled development guidelines and {{ held.n_guidelines }} held-out SCCM guidelines.

**Results** Of {{ t.ALL.n_graded }} graded recommendations in current editions, {{ t.ALL.strong_pct | pct }}% were strong ({{ t.ALL.strong_num }}/{{ t.ALL.strong_den }}; 95% CI {{ t.ALL.strong_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.strong_ci_high) }}), {{ t.ALL.against_pct | pct }}% recommended against an intervention ({{ t.ALL.against_num }}/{{ t.ALL.against_den }}; 95% CI {{ t.ALL.against_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.against_ci_high) }}), and {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% rested on high or moderate certainty evidence ({{ t.ALL.high_moderate_num }}/{{ t.ALL.high_moderate_den }}; 95% CI {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.high_moderate_ci_high) }}). Of {{ t2.ALL.strong.low.den }} strong recommendations with both grades mapped, {{ strong_low }} ({{ strong_low_pct | pct }}%) rested on low or very low certainty evidence. On held-out guidelines, extraction precision was {{ held.precision | p3 }} and recall {{ held.recall | p3 }}; strength and certainty were both correct for {{ held.combined_accuracy | p3 }} of matched recommendations.

**Conclusions and Relevance** In this cross-sectional study, most graded recommendations of three societies rested on low or very low certainty evidence, and about a third of strong recommendations did so too. These are descriptive distributions, not a judgment on individual recommendations; the automated pipeline makes the audit repeatable as guidelines are updated.

## Introduction

Clinical practice guidelines turn evidence into recommendations. The GRADE approach rates two things separately: the certainty of the evidence, from high to very low, and the strength of the recommendation, strong or conditional.[^guyatt2008][^balshem2011] Strength also reflects the balance of benefits and harms, values, resources and feasibility, so a strong recommendation on low certainty evidence is not necessarily wrong.[^andrews2013][^alonsocoello2016] GRADE describes a few paradigmatic situations in which such a recommendation is warranted, such as a life-threatening condition with a plausible benefit and little harm, and expects panels to state which applies.[^andrews2013] Audits of other guideline programs found such pairings frequent and often without a stated justification.[^alexander2014][^alexander2016]

Audits of guideline evidence have shown that few recommendations rest on high-quality evidence. In cardiology, a minority of American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association recommendations were supported by multiple randomized trials,[^tricoci2009] and this share did not increase over the following decade in American or European guidelines.[^fanaroff2019] In infectious diseases, a similar pattern was reported.[^lee2011] In critical care, an analysis of GRADE-based guidelines found that about a third of strong recommendations were paired with evidence that was not of high or moderate quality.[^sims2019] These audits were performed by hand, once, on guidelines that have since been updated.

Critical care and internal medicine guidelines are revised frequently, and new guidelines appear each year. A manual audit becomes outdated as soon as it is published. Large language models can extract structured data from documents,[^gartlehner2024][^hsu2025] which makes a repeatable, living audit of guideline evidence possible,[^elliott2014] provided its extraction accuracy is measured and reported.

We describe the strength, direction and certainty of evidence of graded recommendations in guidelines of ESICM, SCCM and ACP from {{ mf.search.start[:4] }} to {{ mf.search.cutoff_year }}, extracted by a locally run language model from a frozen corpus, and report extraction accuracy separately on development and held-out guidelines.

## Methods

This cross-sectional study used published guidelines only and involved no human participants, so it did not require ethics review or informed consent. We followed the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) reporting guideline where applicable.[^vonelm2007] The analysis plan, outcomes and denominators were specified before the primary tables were computed (Supplement).

### Search and Selection

We ran {{ mf.search.queries | length }} PubMed queries ({{ (mf.search.queries | length) // (mf.corpus.included_by_society | length) }} per society; exact terms in eAppendix 1) for records first published online from {{ mf.search.start }} to {{ mf.search.cutoff }}, and cross-checked the three societies' guideline web pages. The search returned {{ flow.candidates.all }} candidate records{% for source, n in flow.source.items() %}{% if loop.first %} ({% endif %}{{ source | replace("_", " ") }} {{ n }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% else %}){% endif %}{% endfor %}.

Eligible records were clinical practice guidelines led or co-led by ESICM, SCCM or ACP, in any journal, with at least one recommendation graded for both strength and certainty; adult and pediatric guidelines and GRADE-based rapid guidelines were included. We excluded ACP rapid living practice points (certainty without strength), ACP guidance statements and best-practice advice, consensus documents, executive summaries and other derivative papers. A co-published guideline counted once, under the society of the retained publication. {{ mf.search.cutoff_year }} is a partial calendar year.

### Editions

Each guideline belongs to a topic; a newer edition supersedes an older one by an explicit link. Cross-sectional analyses use the {{ flow.current.all }} current editions; time trends use all {{ flow.included.all }} editions. Focused updates that revise only some questions stay current beside the guideline they update, so revised questions count twice (an estimated {{ mf.corpus.focused_update_overlap_max }} graded recommendations at most).

### Extraction and Harmonization

A locally run language model ({{ mf.extractor.model_name }}, digest `{{ mf.extractor.model_digest[:12] }}`) read the full text of each guideline PDF in a single pass and returned each recommendation with its strength and certainty as printed. No guideline text left the local computer. Raw grades were stored as extracted and harmonized on read to GRADE strength (strong or conditional), direction (for or against) and certainty (high, moderate, low or very low); the complete observed mapping is in eTable 1 ({{ raw_harmonized_mapping | length }} distinct raw grades). A statement without a direction is not a graded recommendation, whatever label the model attached. No grade was inferred when the label could not be mapped.

### Within-Guideline Duplicates

A guideline often states a recommendation twice, in a summary list and in the body, sometimes with abbreviations spelled out. {% if dd.applied %}Copies that survived the extractor's own deduplication were removed by a rule fixed before the primary tables were computed. Every pair of statements within one guideline was screened by semantic similarity (BioLORD,[^remy2024] cosine ≥ {{ dd.screen_biolord }}) or character similarity of the normalized text (≥ {{ dd.screen_lexical }}). A similarity threshold alone could not separate copies from sibling recommendations that differ only in population or comparator, so a local language model ({{ dd.adjudication_models | join(", ") }}) judged each of the {{ dd.n_screened_pairs }} screened pairs as the same recommendation restated or as different recommendations. Verdicts were frozen before analysis. Copies were grouped, and one copy per group was kept: the copy with both grades mapped, then the first in the document. This removed {{ dd.n_removed }} statements ({{ dd.n_removed_current_graded }} graded statements in current editions); in {{ dd.n_grade_conflicts }} groups, copies carried different grades. *(Agreement of the verdicts with blind human review: pending; eAppendix 2.)*{% else %}*(No duplicate verdicts configured: copies are not removed.)*{% endif %}

### Outcomes

The unit of analysis was one deduplicated graded recommendation. Primary outcomes were the proportions of recommendations that were strong (for or against), that recommended against an intervention (strong or conditional), and that rested on high or moderate certainty evidence. Strong and against proportions used recommendations with a mapped strength; certainty proportions used those with a mapped certainty. Unmapped grades were reported as counts and not imputed. Denominators therefore differ slightly by outcome: recommendations with a mapped strength, with a mapped certainty, and, for strength by certainty, with both. Pooled estimates (ALL) weigh each recommendation equally and are therefore dominated by SCCM.

### Statistical Analysis

95% CIs came from a guideline-cluster percentile bootstrap ({{ mf.bootstrap.n }} resamples, seed {{ mf.bootstrap.seed }}): guidelines were resampled within each society and, for ALL, within each society and pooled. The intervals describe between-guideline variability in this corpus; they do not account for extraction error. In a sensitivity analysis, each guideline was weighted equally (eTable 2). We performed no hypothesis tests; differences between societies are descriptive.

### Extraction Accuracy

Development accuracy was measured on {{ dev.n_guidelines }} labelled ACP and ESICM guidelines that also informed extractor development, so it is in-sample. Held-out accuracy was measured on {{ held.n_guidelines }} SCCM guidelines drawn at random before any result, labelled blind to the extractor output and not used for tuning. Extracted and labelled recommendations were matched by semantic similarity with one-to-one assignment; a recommendation counted as correctly graded when both its strength (with direction) and its certainty matched the label. Both samples were scored after duplicate removal.

### Reproducibility

All numbers come from one frozen copy of the extraction database (SHA-256 `{{ analysis.inputs.database.sha256[:16] }}…`), rendered by one command without a language model. Checksums of every input and output are listed in the analysis manifest; code, corpus and duplicate verdicts are versioned.

