### Corpus

Of {{ flow.candidates.all }} records screened, {{ flow.included.all }} guidelines were included ({% for s, n in flow.included.items() if s != "all" %}{{ s }} {{ n }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}), {{ flow.current.all }} current and {{ flow.superseded.all }} superseded editions (eFigure 1). The most frequent exclusions were records that were not guidelines ({{ flow.excluded.not_guideline }}), guidelines of other societies ({{ flow.excluded.other_society }}) and derivative publications ({{ flow.excluded.derivative }}); {{ flow.excluded.not_graded }} guidelines graded no recommendation for both strength and certainty. Current editions contained {{ mf.recommendations.n_current }} recommendations after duplicate removal, of which {{ t.ALL.n_graded }} were graded.

### Strength, Direction and Certainty

Overall, {{ t.ALL.strong_pct | pct }}% of graded recommendations were strong (95% CI {{ t.ALL.strong_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.strong_ci_high) }}) and {{ t.ALL.against_pct | pct }}% recommended against an intervention (95% CI {{ t.ALL.against_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.against_ci_high) }}) (Table 1). The share of strong recommendations was {{ t.ACP.strong_pct | pct }}% for ACP (95% CI {{ t.ACP.strong_ci_low | ci(t.ACP.strong_ci_high) }}), {{ t.ESICM.strong_pct | pct }}% for ESICM ({{ t.ESICM.strong_ci_low | ci(t.ESICM.strong_ci_high) }}) and {{ t.SCCM.strong_pct | pct }}% for SCCM ({{ t.SCCM.strong_ci_low | ci(t.SCCM.strong_ci_high) }}).

High or moderate certainty evidence supported {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% of recommendations (95% CI {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.high_moderate_ci_high) }}): {{ t.ACP.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% for ACP, {{ t.ESICM.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% for ESICM and {{ t.SCCM.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% for SCCM. Certainty was low for {{ t2.ALL.certainty.low.pct | pct }}% and very low for {{ t2.ALL.certainty.very_low.pct | pct }}% of recommendations (Table 2; Figure 1).

Certainty and strength were related but not aligned (Figure 2). Of strong recommendations, {{ t2.ALL.strong.high.pct | pct }}% rested on high, {{ t2.ALL.strong.moderate.pct | pct }}% on moderate, {{ t2.ALL.strong.low.pct | pct }}% on low and {{ t2.ALL.strong.very_low.pct | pct }}% on very low certainty evidence. Conversely, {{ t2.ALL.weak.high.n }} of {{ t2.ALL.weak.high.den }} conditional recommendations rested on high certainty evidence.

### Sensitivity Analysis and Trends

Weighting each guideline equally, {{ s1.ALL.strong.mean_pct | pct }}% of recommendations were strong ({{ s1.ALL.strong.diff_pp | pct }} percentage points from the pooled estimate), {{ s1.ALL.against.mean_pct | pct }}% against ({{ s1.ALL.against.diff_pp | pct }}) and {{ s1.ALL.high_moderate.mean_pct | pct }}% high or moderate certainty ({{ s1.ALL.high_moderate.diff_pp | pct }}) (eTable 2). Year-by-society cells were small (Figure 3); we draw no conclusion on temporal change.

### Extraction Accuracy

On {{ dev.n_guidelines }} development guidelines (in-sample), extraction reached F1 {{ dev.candidate.f1 | p3 }} (95% CI {{ dev.candidate.f1_ci | ci3 }}), with precision {{ dev.candidate.precision | p3 }} and recall {{ dev.candidate.recall | p3 }}; both grades were correct for {{ dev.candidate.combined_accuracy | p3 }} ({{ dev.candidate.combined_ci | ci3 }}) of matched recommendations. On {{ held.n_guidelines }} held-out SCCM guidelines, the extractor found {{ held.tp }} of {{ held.tp + held.fn }} labelled recommendations and returned {{ held.fp }} extra statements: precision {{ held.precision | p3 }} ({{ held.precision_ci | ci3 }}), recall {{ held.recall | p3 }} ({{ held.recall_ci | ci3 }}), F1 {{ held.f1 | p3 }}. Strength and certainty were both correct for {{ held.combined_accuracy | p3 }} ({{ held.combined_ci | ci3 }}) of {{ held.n_matched }} matched recommendations (eTable 3).

In an exploratory single-reviewer check of {{ spotcheck_bias.strong.n_guidelines }} unlabelled guidelines, extracted estimates minus reference estimates were, for the share of strong recommendations, {{ spotcheck_bias.strong.diff_pp | pct }} percentage points ({{ spotcheck_bias.strong.diff_ci_low | ci(spotcheck_bias.strong.diff_ci_high) }}) and of high or moderate certainty by {{ spotcheck_bias.high_moderate.diff_pp | pct }} ({{ spotcheck_bias.high_moderate.diff_ci_low | ci(spotcheck_bias.high_moderate.diff_ci_high) }}). Ungraded statements such as best-practice statements were mostly missed ({{ spotcheck_bias.ungraded_share.published_pct | pct }}% extracted vs {{ spotcheck_bias.ungraded_share.truth_pct | pct }}% in the reference), so this study reports graded recommendations only.

## Discussion

In {{ flow.current.all }} current guidelines of three societies, about one in four graded recommendations was strong, and about one in four rested on high or moderate certainty evidence. Most recommendations rested on low or very low certainty evidence, and so did about a third of strong recommendations ({{ strong_low }} of {{ t2.ALL.strong.low.den }}). On held-out guidelines, the extraction pipeline recovered all {{ held.tp }} labelled recommendations and graded most of them correctly.

Our findings agree in direction with earlier audits. In cardiology guidelines, few recommendations were supported by multiple randomized trials, and the share did not rise over a decade.[^fanaroff2019][^tricoci2009] In critical care, Sims and colleagues found a similar share of strong recommendations paired with evidence that was not high or moderate; nearly all strong recommendations on low or very low quality evidence fell within GRADE's paradigmatic situations, most often a life-threatening condition.[^sims2019] Our corpus is broader in societies and years, uses GRADE certainty rather than levels of evidence, and is built so that the same analysis can be rerun on the next edition of each guideline.

A strong recommendation on low certainty evidence is not necessarily inappropriate.[^andrews2013] GRADE accepts it in defined situations, the most common being a life-threatening condition in which an intervention may reduce mortality. We did not assess whether each recommendation met one of these situations, and this study does not judge individual recommendations. It identifies where the share is high, so that panels and readers can check whether each such recommendation states its justification.[^alexander2016]

Differences between societies should be read with care. Weighting each guideline equally, the share of strong recommendations was {{ s1.ESICM.strong.mean_pct | pct }}% for ESICM and {{ s1.ACP.strong.mean_pct | pct }}% for ACP, so pooled differences partly reflect a few large guidelines. ACP rated {% if t2.ACP.certainty.very_low.n == 0 %}no recommendation{% else %}{{ t2.ACP.certainty.very_low.n }} recommendations{% endif %} as very low certainty under its own grading system.[^qaseem2019] Joint SCCM–ESICM guidelines count under one society. SCCM guidelines contributed most recommendations, cover pediatric and adult critical care, and grade differently from ACP, which issues fewer recommendations per guideline. The intervals reflect variability between guidelines and are wide for ACP. We performed no hypothesis tests and do not attribute differences to panel methods.

The main methodological contribution is a reproducible pipeline. Extraction runs on an open-weight model on local hardware without per-use cost; all numbers derive from a frozen database by one command; and accuracy is reported separately on development and held-out guidelines. When a society publishes a new edition, the corpus can be updated and the analysis rerun.[^elliott2014]

### Limitations

This study has limitations. First, accuracy on development guidelines is optimistic because they informed extractor development; the held-out sample comprised only {{ held.n_guidelines }} SCCM guidelines, one of which contributed {{ held.per_guideline | map(attribute='tp') | max }} of the {{ held.tp }} matched recommendations, and its intervals ignore clustering within guidelines. No guideline was double-extracted by humans in full. Second, precision was imperfect: the extractor also returned restatements and statements from comparison tables. Within-guideline duplicates were removed by screening and language-model adjudication, which may miss copies below the screening thresholds or merge closely related recommendations; in {{ dd.n_grade_conflicts }} groups the copies carried different grades, and the kept copy was chosen by rule. Third, ungraded statements were mostly not captured, so the study describes graded recommendations only and cannot report their share. Fourth, residual uncertainty remains in mapping society-specific grading systems to GRADE. Fifth, focused updates stay current beside the guidelines they update, so a few revised questions count twice. Sixth, the corpus is limited to three societies and to guidelines found by our search; {{ mf.search.cutoff_year }} is incomplete. Finally, the spot check of unlabelled guidelines was done by a single reviewer, and in it the extracted shares of strong and of high or moderate certainty recommendations exceeded the reference.

## Conclusions

In this cross-sectional study of ESICM, SCCM and ACP guidelines, most graded recommendations rested on low or very low certainty evidence, as did about a third of strong recommendations. These are descriptive findings, not a judgment that any recommendation implies inappropriate care. An automated, locally run and reproducible extraction pipeline makes it possible to repeat this audit as guidelines are updated.

